# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: namespace-storage-blocks-ci — the CI cannot clone, because one artefact nobody reads fills a free namespace every fortnight
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: issue-namespace-storage

* Symptom

From 14:42 on 2026-08-21, every CI job dies in =get_sources=, on every
runner and every ref, in a handful of seconds:

#+begin_example
error: RPC failed; HTTP 403 curl 22 The requested URL returned error: 403
fatal: expected flush after ref listing
ERROR: Job failed: exit code 128
#+end_example

It took the five release lanes of the =release-1.9.2= tag with it, so
=collect:release= was skipped and the release could not be published. The
same minute, merge-request jobs died the same way — so it is not the tag,
not the release, and not a =.gitlab-ci.yml= change.

* Cause — the namespace is over its storage limit

: namespace  ogamita        plan: free  (10 GB)
: project    clautolisp     job_artifacts_size  23.10 GB

Over the limit, GitLab refuses git operations — which is exactly a 403 on
the fetch, with no message about storage anywhere in the job log. That
silence is why this looked for a while like a CI-token or configuration
fault.

* What is actually in the 23 GB — measured, not guessed

A first pass over the most recent 1500 jobs suggested the bulk was old
history, and this ticket said so. *That was wrong*, and it pointed at the
wrong fix. A full walk of all 32 578 jobs on 2026-08-21 says:

| job                    | jobs | artefacts |
|------------------------+------+-----------|
| =documentation=        |  552 |   9.70 GB |
| =collect:release=      |    8 |   3.95 GB |
| =build:alfe:sbcl=      |  925 |   3.35 GB |
| =release:linux:x86-64= |   32 |   1.99 GB |
| =release:linux:arm64=  |   30 |   0.79 GB |
| =release:darwin:arm64= |   26 |   0.69 GB |
| everything else        |    — |   2.63 GB |

Expiry *does* work: sampled jobs older than about a fortnight hold only
their trace, no archive. So this is not accumulated history at all — it is
roughly *twelve days of production*. Over the twelve days the API still had
archives for:

: documentation      175 runs   8.99 GB    51 MB each
: build:alfe:sbcl    207 runs   2.74 GB    13 MB each

=documentation= alone is 39% of the total, and *nothing consumes it*:
=deploy:documentation= needs the tag lane's =release:documentation=, and
=pages= needs the benchmarks. Its dist/ exists so a human can look at the
rendered manuals after a doc change. It kept them a week, at 51 MB per
merge-request and per master pipeline.

* The permanent cost, which is real but is NOT what filled the disk

=collect:release= carries =expire_in: never= deliberately, because a
published Release LINKS AT its artefacts: with an expiry, every release
turns into dead links some weeks later
(release-asset-links-use-artifacts-file.issue, and =make check-release=
enforces the =never=). So every release costs about a gigabyte for ever:

: 954 MB  release-1.8.49      (published, permanent)
: 954 MB  release-1.8.47      (published, permanent)
: 952 MB  release-1.9.0       (published, permanent)
: 683 MB  release-1.8.46      (expires 2026-09-17)
: 410 MB  two master runs     (expiring today)

That is 2.86 GB of genuinely permanent storage — about a tenth of the
allowance per release, which is worth designing for, but it is *not* what
took the namespace down. Three releases in one day looked like the culprit
and were not.

* Fix

1. *Shorten the two expiries that dominate* — done in this branch:
   =documentation= 1 week → *1 day* (no consumer at all), =build:alfe:sbcl=
   14 days → *3 days* (its consumers =needs:= it inside the same pipeline).
   Together those are 11.7 GB of the 23 GB; at the new expiries they settle
   at roughly 1.3 GB and 0.6 GB. This is the change that stops it recurring:
   without it the namespace refills in a fortnight whatever is deleted today.

2. *Purge the existing job artefacts, keeping every =collect:release=* —
   *needs pjb's word, and this is the only open question in this ticket.*
   Frees *19.15 GB*, leaving *3.98 GB* of the 10 GB allowed. Every object it
   removes already carries an expiry of 14–90 days and has no consumer: it
   brings forward what GitLab will do anyway. Script:
   =/home/claude/.claude/jobs/2b87a7b6/tmp/purge-artifacts.py= (dry-run by
   default, =--apply= to delete, =KEEP_NAMES = {"collect:release"}=).

   *This is enough on its own.* An earlier draft of this ticket asked
   whether release-1.9.0's 952 MB should be sacrificed — it is superseded,
   its Windows products do not work, and deleting it would turn its 15 asset
   links into 404s. With (2) landing at 3.98 GB, that question does not
   arise. Leave the published releases alone.

3. *Longer term, if releases keep being cut at this rate*: the combined
   =-all.zip= / =-all.tar.bz2= are 276 MB and 246 MB of each 952 MB and
   duplicate the per-target archives byte for byte — dropping them halves
   the permanent cost per release, but pjb named the ten artefacts a release
   must publish (release-artefact-set-incomplete.issue), so that is his call
   too. The root fix is publishing release assets outside job artefacts (the
   Package Registry, or a release-assets project), which removes the expiry
   tension entirely.

* Note

The job log says only "403"; the project statistics say 23.10 GB; the
namespace says "free". Nothing connects them. Worth writing down because the
next person to meet this will see a 403 on a git fetch and go looking at
tokens, which is where the first hour went here — and because the first
measurement, taken over a 1500-job window that happened to cut across the
expiry horizon, gave a confidently wrong answer that would have led to
deleting a published release's assets for nothing.
